\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/data-sources}

A data source is whoever actually produced the numbers: CoinGecko,
Zapper.fi, Yahoo Finance, a CSV dump, a proprietary feed.  Retrievals
point at a source, and candlesticks point at a retrieval, so every bar
can be traced all the way down.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{data-source}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \texttt{name},
\lispId{data-source-kind-id}, and \lispId{data-source-kind}.  The accessors
have the obvious names.  \lispId{data-source-kind} is the related
\lispId{data-source-kind} object, or \texttt{nil}.
\lispId{data-source-kind-name} on a source is the kind's canonical name,
or \texttt{nil}.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-data-source}

Find or create the data source with the given name and return it.
\texttt{:kind} is a \texttt{data-source-kind} or a name such as
\texttt{"api"}; when given, it fills in a missing kind on an existing
source.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(make-data-source \lispVar{name} \&key \lispVar{kind})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{data-source-by-name}

The data source with the given name, or \texttt{nil}.  This does not
create a row.

\lispFunctionSubsection{data-source-id-for}

The database \textsc{Uuid} of the data source called \lispVar{name},
created if it does not exist yet.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(data-source-id-for \lispVar{name} \&optional \lispVar{kind})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{all-data-sources}

Every data source, alphabetically by name.
